diagnosis_text:
The inter-VLAN “ramp” between the 172.16.10.0/24 floor and the 172.16.30.0/24 floor is operating normally. End-to-end tests show cross-floor reachability is intact: from pc5 (172.16.10.5) to upstairs hosts pc12/pc13/pc14 (172.16.30.12/.13/.14) pings succeed with 0% loss and sub‑2 ms RTTs (e.g., pc5→pc13: 5/5 received, avg 1.26 ms; pc5→pc14: 5/5 received, avg 1.55 ms). The topology-wide matrix also confirms multiple hosts in 10.x/20.x/30.x can reach each other across VLANs without loss. The “big switch” uplinks that stitch floors together are up; for example, mswitch0 eth2 (uplink toward switch1) reports Link detected: yes at 10 Gbps full‑duplex.

Given the present evidence, there is no active misconfiguration or failure on the L2/L3 interconnect (trunks, VLANs, or inter‑VLAN routing). The earlier timeouts were likely transient or endpoint-local (e.g., stale ARP/neighbor entry, temporary host firewall, or brief link flap) and have cleared.

Root cause: No current network fault; the earlier outage appears to have been transient or local to the reporting endpoint.

Corrective fix: No changes required at this time. Recommend the user re-test; if symptoms recur, capture evidence (timestamps, target IPs), and on pc5 check default gateway, ARP table, and firewall, while we gather switch/router logs for the timeframe to isolate any intermittent link or trunk events.
